      "costUsd": 0.008568000000000001,
      "pronunciationText": "In general, if M goes in at the end of each period, with interest at r per period, then the future value after n periods is M times one plus r to the n, minus one, all over r. The ratio of the series is one plus r, not r, so its bottom line, one plus r minus one, is just r. The formula has conditions. Payments come at the end of each period. The future value is read at the end of the last period, straight after the last payment. The account starts at zero, r is the rate per period, and n counts the periods."
